<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulu Nadu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tulu Nadu is the region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> where the Tulu language is spoken, comprising the Dakshina <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and part of Kasaragod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. It was ruled by the Alupa dynasty for much of the first and early second millennia CE and later absorbed into Vijayanagara. It has distinctive traditions such as Bhuta Kola spirit worship and Yakshagana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tulu-nadu.jpg" alt="Tulu Nadu" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr anonymousMr · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulunadu_Region.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
